{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LnJkdMJexD3UqCeZ6bwDs6","type":"tool_result","content":"191:The proposed pair scores 0.247 against 0.220 for the simple pair, with non-overlapping per-seed ranges (0.243--0.251 vs.\\ 0.220), and also shows a larger skill over random-$k$ selection (0.132 vs.\\ 0.100). % @evidence[[analysis/search_metrics.json#02]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#result.top_program_01]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#result.top_program_03]]\nThe proposed pair scores 0.247 against 0.220 for the simple pair, with non-overlapping per-seed ranges (0.243--0.251 vs.\\ 0.220), and also shows a larger skill over random-$k$ selection (0.132 vs.\\ 0.100). % @evidence[[analysis/search_metrics.json#02]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#result.top_program_01]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#result.top_program_03]]\nBecause several components change together and the two seeds share one held-out set, this licenses only the statement that the richer configuration was better on that partition; it does not attribute the gain to the transform, the wrapper selector or the stack. The pair extended with a latent-skill member and a latent A-optimal selector produced the same four metrics as the proposed pair to all reported digits. The extension is kept only if it improves cross-validation error, which is consistent with it being inactive, but no diagnostic recording the choice was retained, so the cause is unknown, and we treat this configuration as a null result. The sealed evaluation was run on one of these two configurations; the exact tie means the retained scores cannot say which (Supplementary Information).\n\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\\label{sec:limits}\n\\paragraph{What is and is not shown.} The sealed result supports a bounded statement: on family-disjoint held-out models from three pinned leaderboards, a history-fitted selector--imputer pair predicts unrun benchmark scores with a mean relative error reduction (skill) of 0.434 against column means, and the improvement is not an artefact of the imputer's marginals since permuting the observations erases it. The evidence does not show that cross-benchmark structure is \\emph{exploited by selection}; it shows that observed scores help, and that a random subset with a ridge imputer comes within a skill of 0.016 of the pair.\n\n\\paragraph{Joint scoring and missing ablations.} Selector and imputer are scored together. No controlled experiment varied one while holding the other fixed, so the contributions of the rank-Gauss transform, the wrapper selector, the stack and the clipping are unattributed. The development-partition comparison changes many components at once. A fair selector test would pair each selector with each imputer; we did not run it.\n\n\\paragraph{Uncertainty.} Reported values are seed means. The seeds vary only the method's internal randomness (fold assignments), not the held-out models, baselines or controls, so the 0.006 spread across seeds understates sampling uncertainty over models. Held-out sets are small for MTEB (37 models) and HELM Lite (25), and the held-out groups are few (25 and 7). The 0.016 margin over random-$k$ should be viewed as indistinguishable from zero until a resampling-based interval over held-out models is available; we did not compute one, and we make no significance claim.\n\n\\paragraph{Scope.} Three leaderboards, of which two contain generative language models in small numbers of benchmarks (six and ten) and one contains text-embedding models; scales differ (accuracies, win rates, F1). Family-disjoint splitting was defined by name and content heuristics. Selection is non-adaptive; adaptive stopping or sequential selection could change the picture. Missingness in leaderboards is not random (strong models are run on more benchmarks), which violates the usual missing-at-random assumption of EM-based imputation \\citep{Rubin1976Inference}. The run-time guards in Section~\\ref{sec:impl} make behaviour hardware-dependent.\n\n\\paragraph{Unperformed analyses.} We did not run a theory check of the trace objective, a component ablation, per-family error breakdowns, comparisons with low-rank matrix completion or latent-factor imputers as full alternatives, or other leaderboards. These are not claimed.\n\n\\paragraph{Threats to validity.} (1) \\emph{Leakage through naming.} Family-disjointness relies on name normalisation, a family key and a content hash; a model re-released under an unrecognised name could cross the split. (2) \\emph{Metric scale.} Errors are in history standard deviations of each benchmark, which makes a benchmark with a narrow history spread (e.g.\\ one saturated by strong models) look harder to predict than one with a wide spread. (3) \\emph{Heterogeneous coverage.} Leaderboard missingness is related to model strength and recency; selected benchmarks that are well covered in the history may be missing for new models. (4) \\emph{Configuration identity and freezing.} The configuration was chosen on the development partition before the sealed evaluation, but no artifact records which of the two tied configurations the sealed run used or that it was run only once; the sealed numbers are not selected on, but the development partition is from the same MTEB snapshot, so the MTEB result is closer to the development distribution than the other two leaderboards, which were not used to choose among configurations. (5) \\emph{Implementation coupling.} The result is for one concrete implementation, including its tuning grids and wall-clock guards; a different implementation of the same design could behave differently.\n\n\\paragraph{Practical reading.} For an evaluator with a comparable history, a handful of well-covered benchmarks and a reasonable imputer already remove a large part of the column-mean error, further benchmarks buy progressively less, and an unavoidable residual of about 0.64 history standard deviations remains on MTEB at $k{=}20$.\n\n\\section{Conclusion}\nWe posed the cold-start question of how well $k$ benchmark runs predict the remaining scores of a model from an unseen family, and evaluated a rank-Gauss, EM-Gaussian, wrapper-selected, NNLS-stacked selector--imputer family (one member of it, not identifiable from the retained evidence) on three leaderboards with family-disjoint held-out models. The sealed evaluation completed and qualified. Pooled nRMSE is 0.562 and skill against column means is 0.434, with a clear gap to permuted controls; on MTEB the error decreases from 0.787 to 0.638 as $k$ rises from 2 to 20 with sharply diminishing returns, and error levels differ across leaderboards. The margin over random-$k$ selection with a ridge imputer is only 0.016 and cannot be assigned to selection or imputation, and the retained evidence suggests that a leave-one-out greedy selector can fail to beat random subsets. The main unresolved questions are the isolated value of selection, uncertainty over held-out models, and behaviour beyond these three leaderboards; each needs additional controlled experiments that this study did not perform.\n\n\\clearpage\n\\bibliography{references}\n\\bibliographystyle{paper}\n\n\\clearpage\n\\appendix\n\\section{Supplementary Information}\n\n\\subsection{Evaluation protocol details}\nTable~\\ref{tab:data} lists the data partitions used by the sealed evaluation, taken from the evaluator's dataset manifests. % @evidence[[analysis/protocol_facts.json#final]] @evidence[[scripts/extract_protocol_facts.py]]\nGroup counts are numbers of family groups after union-find merging. The model-development partition (MTEB, 280 columns, 131 history models, 32 held-out models, revision identical to the sealed MTEB revision) uses a disjoint set of groups. % @evidence[[analysis/protocol_facts.json#search]]\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Sealed evaluation partitions.}\n\\label{tab:data}\n\\small\n\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{4pt}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n\\toprule\nLeaderboard & Revision & Cols. & History & Held-out & Groups (h/o) & $k$ grid\\\\\n\\midrule\nMTEB results & 6dabbcc & 297 & 124 & 37 & 92 / 25 & 2, 5, 10, 20\\\\\nOpen LLM Leaderboard & 9c09a7c & 6 & 3371 & 300 & 2314 / 266 & 1, 2, 3\\\\\nHELM Lite & v1.13.0 & 10 & 66 & 25 & 16 / 7 & 2, 3, 5\\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\noindent Score definitions: MTEB uses each task's main score averaged over the entries of the first available split (test, validation, dev, train), taken from the model revision directory with the most task files; Open LLM Leaderboard uses raw scores of IFEval, BBH, MATH Level 5, GPQA, MUSR and MMLU-PRO, dropping rows with a missing score; HELM Lite uses the ten scenario columns of the core-scenarios table, excluding the mean-win-rate column. Held-out MTEB and HELM Lite models may lack some scores; those cells are not scored. Per-seed pooled scores of the sealed evaluation were 0.4330 (seed 201), 0.4273 (202) and 0.4328 (203), taking 157.4, 160.6 and 161.0 seconds for 14 episodes each. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.evidence.episodes]]\n\n\\subsection{Method specification and hyperparameters}\nTable~\\ref{tab:hyper} collects the constants of the pair described in Section~\\ref{sec:impl}; they are read from the entry-point source of the proposed-pair source copy (\\texttt{src/program.py}: the EM, greedy-selection, wrapper-selection, stacking and prediction routines). This copy is not verified to be the exact tree evaluated in the sealed run (see below), so the constants describe the proposed pair, not a confirmed record of the sealed configuration. % @evidence[[source_evidence/config_proposed/program.py]]\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Constants of the proposed pair, read from its source copy (not verified as the exact sealed-evaluated tree).}\n\\label{tab:hyper}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lp{0.5\\textwidth}}\n\\toprule\nComponent & Setting\\\\\n\\midrule\nEM & shrinkage 0.02, $\\le 60$ iterations, tol.\\ $10^{-4}$, jitter $10^{-4}$, 25-s budget\\\\\nCross-validation & 5 folds over history models\\\\\nCovariance shrinkage grid & 0, 0.05, 0.15, 0.3\\\\\nFactor-rank grid & 2, 4, 6, 10, full\\\\\nRidge $\\lambda$ grid (Gaussian conditional mean) & 0.003, 0.03, 0.1, 0.3\\\\\nSelection candidates & 4 trace-greedy settings, 2 wrapper settings (coverage exponent 0 or 1)\\\\\nWrapper & $\\lambda=0.1$, shrinkage 0.05, $\\le 250$ candidates per step\\\\\nStack base predictors & Gaussian conditional means (shrink 0, 0.15, 0.3), ridge ($\\lambda=5$), kNN (5, 15)\\\\\nStack regularisation grid & 0, 0.05, 0.25, 1, 5, $10^{5}$\\\\\nClipping & z-scores at $\\pm 8$; outputs to the history range\\\\\nWall-clock guards & optional candidates 70 s; full tuning 150 s; stacking 210 s\\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\noindent The latent-skill extension (third row of Table~\\ref{tab:search}) adds a low-rank model $T_{ij}\\approx g_j(u_i\\cdot v_j+b_j)$ with a monotone piecewise-linear link $g_j$ per benchmark, Huber-likelihood MAP inference of a new model's latent vector with a Laplace covariance, an additional stack member retained only if cross-validation improves, and a latent A-optimal selector candidate. Its metrics equal those of the proposed pair (Table~\\ref{tab:search}); the extension took roughly 331--334 seconds per search seed against 141--142 seconds for the proposed pair. % @evidence[[analysis/search_metrics.json#03]]\nThe longer run time indicates that the extra code ran, but its influence on outputs is not established. The final-evaluation record lists a hash of the evaluated source tree; we were not able to match this hash to a single source directory, and the tie above means the retained scores cannot discriminate the two. The main text describes the shared stack, and the extension is described here because the sealed run may have used it. The simple pair used 95--97 seconds per search seed. % @evidence[[analysis/search_metrics.json#01]]\n\n\\subsection{Extra evidence tables}\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Development-partition per-seed scores and durations (seconds, two seeds each).}\n\\label{tab:seeds}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\nConfiguration & Score (seed 11) & Score (seed 12) & Time (seed 11) & Time (seed 12)\\\\\n\\midrule\nSimple pair & 0.2201 & 0.2197 & 95.4 & 97.3\\\\\nProposed pair & 0.2429 & 0.2506 & 140.9 & 142.2\\\\\nProposed + latent member & 0.2429 & 0.2506 & 331.2 & 334.3\\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n% @evidence[[analysis/search_metrics.json]]\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Unperformed analyses}\nThe following were not run and are not claimed: component ablations (the ablation directory of the run is missing); empirical verification of the trace objective as expected error; bootstrap or model-resampling intervals; per-leaderboard $k$ curves for Open LLM Leaderboard and HELM Lite as retained metrics; baseline $\\nrmse$ values per budget; additional leaderboards such as LiveBench, BigCodeBench or AlpacaEval, which the task description lists as optional.\n\n\\subsection{Reproducibility, data and licences}\nEntry point: a single Python file exposing a \\texttt{handle} request function (operations \\texttt{reset}, \\texttt{load\\_history}, \\texttt{select}, \\texttt{predict}); dependencies are NumPy only; behaviour is deterministic given the seed except for the wall-clock guards. Figures and tables in this paper come from \\texttt{scripts/build\\_figures.py} (Figure~\\ref{fig:main}), \\texttt{scripts/extract\\_protocol\\_facts.py} (Table~\\ref{tab:data}), \\texttt{scripts/extract\\_search\\_metrics.py} (Tables~\\ref{tab:search} and~\\ref{tab:seeds}); the sealed metrics are read from the evaluation registry. Data sources are public: MTEB results (CC0-1.0), Open LLM Leaderboard contents (no dataset licence declared on its card), and HELM Lite release v1.13.0 (scenario data under per-scenario licences, not individually verified). Literature retrieval used only titles from the starter literature and standard methodology references; no material from the excluded publication was knowingly accessed.\n\n\\subsection{LLM Usage}\nLarge language models were used for research hypothesis generation, for implementing and running the compared configurations, and for drafting and editing this manuscript. The selector--imputer designs, code, experiments and the text were produced by an automated LLM-driven research workflow, with evaluator-owned scoring that the workflow could not modify. All reported numbers were read from evaluator outputs and checked against the evidence registry; the human role was limited to specifying the task and evaluation setting. References were retrieved from bibliographic databases and checked against their","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"312ca5fd-a39a-43df-9864-a2f2feae8418","uuid":"1b846297-7f7b-4f17-b003-c45849bcf189","timestamp":"2026-11-11T13:02:55.088Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"191:The proposed pair scores 0.247 against 0.220 for the simple pair, with non-overlapping per-seed ranges (0.243--0.251 vs.\\ 0.220), and also shows a larger skill over random-$k$ selection (0.132 vs.\\ 0.100). % @evidence[[analysis/search_metrics.json#02]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#result.top_program_01]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#result.top_program_03]]\nThe proposed pair scores 0.247 against 0.220 for the simple pair, with non-overlapping per-seed ranges (0.243--0.251 vs.\\ 0.220), and also shows a larger skill over random-$k$ selection (0.132 vs.\\ 0.100). % @evidence[[analysis/search_metrics.json#02]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#result.top_program_01]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#result.top_program_03]]\nBecause several components change together and the two seeds share one held-out set, this licenses only the statement that the richer configuration was better on that partition; it does not attribute the gain to the transform, the wrapper selector or the stack. The pair extended with a latent-skill member and a latent A-optimal selector produced the same four metrics as the proposed pair to all reported digits. The extension is kept only if it improves cross-validation error, which is consistent with it being inactive, but no diagnostic recording the choice was retained, so the cause is unknown, and we treat this configuration as a null result. The sealed evaluation was run on one of these two configurations; the exact tie means the retained scores cannot say which (Supplementary Information).\n\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\\label{sec:limits}\n\\paragraph{What is and is not shown.} The sealed result supports a bounded statement: on family-disjoint held-out models from three pinned leaderboards, a history-fitted selector--imputer pair predicts unrun benchmark scores with a mean relative error reduction (skill) of 0.434 against column means, and the improvement is not an artefact of the imputer's marginals since permuting the observations erases it. The evidence does not show that cross-benchmark structure is \\emph{exploited by selection}; it shows that observed scores help, and that a random subset with a ridge imputer comes within a skill of 0.016 of the pair.\n\n\\paragraph{Joint scoring and missing ablations.} Selector and imputer are scored together. No controlled experiment varied one while holding the other fixed, so the contributions of the rank-Gauss transform, the wrapper selector, the stack and the clipping are unattributed. The development-partition comparison changes many components at once. A fair selector test would pair each selector with each imputer; we did not run it.\n\n\\paragraph{Uncertainty.} Reported values are seed means. The seeds vary only the method's internal randomness (fold assignments), not the held-out models, baselines or controls, so the 0.006 spread across seeds understates sampling uncertainty over models. Held-out sets are small for MTEB (37 models) and HELM Lite (25), and the held-out groups are few (25 and 7). The 0.016 margin over random-$k$ should be viewed as indistinguishable from zero until a resampling-based interval over held-out models is available; we did not compute one, and we make no significance claim.\n\n\\paragraph{Scope.} Three leaderboards, of which two contain generative language models in small numbers of benchmarks (six and ten) and one contains text-embedding models; scales differ (accuracies, win rates, F1). Family-disjoint splitting was defined by name and content heuristics. Selection is non-adaptive; adaptive stopping or sequential selection could change the picture. Missingness in leaderboards is not random (strong models are run on more benchmarks), which violates the usual missing-at-random assumption of EM-based imputation \\citep{Rubin1976Inference}. The run-time guards in Section~\\ref{sec:impl} make behaviour hardware-dependent.\n\n\\paragraph{Unperformed analyses.} We did not run a theory check of the trace objective, a component ablation, per-family error breakdowns, comparisons with low-rank matrix completion or latent-factor imputers as full alternatives, or other leaderboards. These are not claimed.\n\n\\paragraph{Threats to validity.} (1) \\emph{Leakage through naming.} Family-disjointness relies on name normalisation, a family key and a content hash; a model re-released under an unrecognised name could cross the split. (2) \\emph{Metric scale.} Errors are in history standard deviations of each benchmark, which makes a benchmark with a narrow history spread (e.g.\\ one saturated by strong models) look harder to predict than one with a wide spread. (3) \\emph{Heterogeneous coverage.} Leaderboard missingness is related to model strength and recency; selected benchmarks that are well covered in the history may be missing for new models. (4) \\emph{Configuration identity and freezing.} The configuration was chosen on the development partition before the sealed evaluation, but no artifact records which of the two tied configurations the sealed run used or that it was run only once; the sealed numbers are not selected on, but the development partition is from the same MTEB snapshot, so the MTEB result is closer to the development distribution than the other two leaderboards, which were not used to choose among configurations. (5) \\emph{Implementation coupling.} The result is for one concrete implementation, including its tuning grids and wall-clock guards; a different implementation of the same design could behave differently.\n\n\\paragraph{Practical reading.} For an evaluator with a comparable history, a handful of well-covered benchmarks and a reasonable imputer already remove a large part of the column-mean error, further benchmarks buy progressively less, and an unavoidable residual of about 0.64 history standard deviations remains on MTEB at $k{=}20$.\n\n\\section{Conclusion}\nWe posed the cold-start question of how well $k$ benchmark runs predict the remaining scores of a model from an unseen family, and evaluated a rank-Gauss, EM-Gaussian, wrapper-selected, NNLS-stacked selector--imputer family (one member of it, not identifiable from the retained evidence) on three leaderboards with family-disjoint held-out models. The sealed evaluation completed and qualified. Pooled nRMSE is 0.562 and skill against column means is 0.434, with a clear gap to permuted controls; on MTEB the error decreases from 0.787 to 0.638 as $k$ rises from 2 to 20 with sharply diminishing returns, and error levels differ across leaderboards. The margin over random-$k$ selection with a ridge imputer is only 0.016 and cannot be assigned to selection or imputation, and the retained evidence suggests that a leave-one-out greedy selector can fail to beat random subsets. The main unresolved questions are the isolated value of selection, uncertainty over held-out models, and behaviour beyond these three leaderboards; each needs additional controlled experiments that this study did not perform.\n\n\\clearpage\n\\bibliography{references}\n\\bibliographystyle{paper}\n\n\\clearpage\n\\appendix\n\\section{Supplementary Information}\n\n\\subsection{Evaluation protocol details}\nTable~\\ref{tab:data} lists the data partitions used by the sealed evaluation, taken from the evaluator's dataset manifests. % @evidence[[analysis/protocol_facts.json#final]] @evidence[[scripts/extract_protocol_facts.py]]\nGroup counts are numbers of family groups after union-find merging. The model-development partition (MTEB, 280 columns, 131 history models, 32 held-out models, revision identical to the sealed MTEB revision) uses a disjoint set of groups. % @evidence[[analysis/protocol_facts.json#search]]\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Sealed evaluation partitions.}\n\\label{tab:data}\n\\small\n\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{4pt}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n\\toprule\nLeaderboard & Revision & Cols. & History & Held-out & Groups (h/o) & $k$ grid\\\\\n\\midrule\nMTEB results & 6dabbcc & 297 & 124 & 37 & 92 / 25 & 2, 5, 10, 20\\\\\nOpen LLM Leaderboard & 9c09a7c & 6 & 3371 & 300 & 2314 / 266 & 1, 2, 3\\\\\nHELM Lite & v1.13.0 & 10 & 66 & 25 & 16 / 7 & 2, 3, 5\\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\noindent Score definitions: MTEB uses each task's main score averaged over the entries of the first available split (test, validation, dev, train), taken from the model revision directory with the most task files; Open LLM Leaderboard uses raw scores of IFEval, BBH, MATH Level 5, GPQA, MUSR and MMLU-PRO, dropping rows with a missing score; HELM Lite uses the ten scenario columns of the core-scenarios table, excluding the mean-win-rate column. Held-out MTEB and HELM Lite models may lack some scores; those cells are not scored. Per-seed pooled scores of the sealed evaluation were 0.4330 (seed 201), 0.4273 (202) and 0.4328 (203), taking 157.4, 160.6 and 161.0 seconds for 14 episodes each. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.evidence.episodes]]\n\n\\subsection{Method specification and hyperparameters}\nTable~\\ref{tab:hyper} collects the constants of the pair described in Section~\\ref{sec:impl}; they are read from the entry-point source of the proposed-pair source copy (\\texttt{src/program.py}: the EM, greedy-selection, wrapper-selection, stacking and prediction routines). This copy is not verified to be the exact tree evaluated in the sealed run (see below), so the constants describe the proposed pair, not a confirmed record of the sealed configuration. % @evidence[[source_evidence/config_proposed/program.py]]\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Constants of the proposed pair, read from its source copy (not verified as the exact sealed-evaluated tree).}\n\\label{tab:hyper}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lp{0.5\\textwidth}}\n\\toprule\nComponent & Setting\\\\\n\\midrule\nEM & shrinkage 0.02, $\\le 60$ iterations, tol.\\ $10^{-4}$, jitter $10^{-4}$, 25-s budget\\\\\nCross-validation & 5 folds over history models\\\\\nCovariance shrinkage grid & 0, 0.05, 0.15, 0.3\\\\\nFactor-rank grid & 2, 4, 6, 10, full\\\\\nRidge $\\lambda$ grid (Gaussian conditional mean) & 0.003, 0.03, 0.1, 0.3\\\\\nSelection candidates & 4 trace-greedy settings, 2 wrapper settings (coverage exponent 0 or 1)\\\\\nWrapper & $\\lambda=0.1$, shrinkage 0.05, $\\le 250$ candidates per step\\\\\nStack base predictors & Gaussian conditional means (shrink 0, 0.15, 0.3), ridge ($\\lambda=5$), kNN (5, 15)\\\\\nStack regularisation grid & 0, 0.05, 0.25, 1, 5, $10^{5}$\\\\\nClipping & z-scores at $\\pm 8$; outputs to the history range\\\\\nWall-clock guards & optional candidates 70 s; full tuning 150 s; stacking 210 s\\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\noindent The latent-skill extension (third row of Table~\\ref{tab:search}) adds a low-rank model $T_{ij}\\approx g_j(u_i\\cdot v_j+b_j)$ with a monotone piecewise-linear link $g_j$ per benchmark, Huber-likelihood MAP inference of a new model's latent vector with a Laplace covariance, an additional stack member retained only if cross-validation improves, and a latent A-optimal selector candidate. Its metrics equal those of the proposed pair (Table~\\ref{tab:search}); the extension took roughly 331--334 seconds per search seed against 141--142 seconds for the proposed pair. % @evidence[[analysis/search_metrics.json#03]]\nThe longer run time indicates that the extra code ran, but its influence on outputs is not established. The final-evaluation record lists a hash of the evaluated source tree; we were not able to match this hash to a single source directory, and the tie above means the retained scores cannot discriminate the two. The main text describes the shared stack, and the extension is described here because the sealed run may have used it. The simple pair used 95--97 seconds per search seed. % @evidence[[analysis/search_metrics.json#01]]\n\n\\subsection{Extra evidence tables}\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Development-partition per-seed scores and durations (seconds, two seeds each).}\n\\label{tab:seeds}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\nConfiguration & Score (seed 11) & Score (seed 12) & Time (seed 11) & Time (seed 12)\\\\\n\\midrule\nSimple pair & 0.2201 & 0.2197 & 95.4 & 97.3\\\\\nProposed pair & 0.2429 & 0.2506 & 140.9 & 142.2\\\\\nProposed + latent member & 0.2429 & 0.2506 & 331.2 & 334.3\\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n% @evidence[[analysis/search_metrics.json]]\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Unperformed analyses}\nThe following were not run and are not claimed: component ablations (the ablation directory of the run is missing); empirical verification of the trace objective as expected error; bootstrap or model-resampling intervals; per-leaderboard $k$ curves for Open LLM Leaderboard and HELM Lite as retained metrics; baseline $\\nrmse$ values per budget; additional leaderboards such as LiveBench, BigCodeBench or AlpacaEval, which the task description lists as optional.\n\n\\subsection{Reproducibility, data and licences}\nEntry point: a single Python file exposing a \\texttt{handle} request function (operations \\texttt{reset}, \\texttt{load\\_history}, \\texttt{select}, \\texttt{predict}); dependencies are NumPy only; behaviour is deterministic given the seed except for the wall-clock guards. Figures and tables in this paper come from \\texttt{scripts/build\\_figures.py} (Figure~\\ref{fig:main}), \\texttt{scripts/extract\\_protocol\\_facts.py} (Table~\\ref{tab:data}), \\texttt{scripts/extract\\_search\\_metrics.py} (Tables~\\ref{tab:search} and~\\ref{tab:seeds}); the sealed metrics are read from the evaluation registry. Data sources are public: MTEB results (CC0-1.0), Open LLM Leaderboard contents (no dataset licence declared on its card), and HELM Lite release v1.13.0 (scenario data under per-scenario licences, not individually verified). Literature retrieval used only titles from the starter literature and standard methodology references; no material from the excluded publication was knowingly accessed.\n\n\\subsection{LLM Usage}\nLarge language models were used for research hypothesis generation, for implementing and running the compared configurations, and for drafting and editing this manuscript. The selector--imputer designs, code, experiments and the text were produced by an automated LLM-driven research workflow, with evaluator-owned scoring that the workflow could not modify. All reported numbers were read from evaluator outputs and checked against the evidence registry; the human role was limited to specifying the task and evaluation setting. References were retrieved from bibliographic databases and checked against their","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
